#: ../pwntools/docs/source/gdb.rst:12
msgid ":mod:`pwnlib.gdb` --- Working with GDB"
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.gdb:1
msgid ""
"During exploit development, it is frequently useful to debug the target "
"binary under GDB."
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.gdb:4
msgid ""
"Pwntools makes this easy-to-do with a handful of helper routines, "
"designed to make your exploit-debug-update cycles much faster."
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.gdb:8
msgid "Useful Functions"
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.gdb:10
msgid ":func:`attach` - Attach to an existing process"
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.gdb:11
msgid ""
":func:`debug` - Start a new process under a debugger, stopped at the "
"first instruction"
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.gdb:12
msgid ""
":func:`debug_shellcode` - Build a binary with the provided shellcode, and"
" start it under a debugger"
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.gdb:15
msgid "Debugging Tips"
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.gdb:17
msgid ""
"The :func:`attach` and :func:`debug` functions will likely be your bread "
"and butter for debugging."
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.gdb:20
msgid ""
"Both allow you to provide a script to pass to GDB when it is started, so "
"that it can automatically set your breakpoints."
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.gdb:24
msgid "Attaching to Processes"
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.gdb:26
msgid ""
"To attach to an existing process, just use :func:`attach`.  It is "
"surprisingly versatile, and can attach to a :class:`.process` for simple "
"binaries, or will automatically find the correct process to attach to for"
" a forking server, if given a :class:`.remote` object."
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.gdb:32
msgid "Spawning New Processes"
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.gdb:34
msgid ""
"Attaching to processes with :func:`attach` is useful, but the state the "
"process is in may vary.  If you need to attach to a process very early, "
"and debug it from the very first instruction (or even the start of "
"``main``), you instead should use :func:`debug`."
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.gdb:39
msgid ""
"When you use :func:`debug`, the return value is a :class:`.tube` object "
"that you interact with exactly like normal."
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.gdb:43
msgid "Using GDB Python API"
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.gdb:45
msgid ""
"GDB provides Python API, which is documented at "
"https://sourceware.org/gdb/onlinedocs/gdb/Python-API.html. Pwntools "
"allows you to call it right from the exploit, without having to write a "
"gdbscript. This is useful for inspecting program state, e.g. asserting "
"that leaked values are correct, or that certain packets trigger a "
"particular code path or put the heap in a desired state."
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.gdb:52
msgid ""
"Pass ``api=True`` to :func:`attach` or :func:`debug` in order to enable "
"GDB Python API access. Pwntools will then connect to GDB using RPyC "
"library: https://rpyc.readthedocs.io/en/latest/."
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.gdb:56
msgid ""
"At the moment this is an experimental feature with the following "
"limitations:"
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.gdb:58
msgid "Only Python 3 is supported."
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.gdb:60
msgid ""
"Well, technically that's not quite true. The real limitation is that your"
" GDB's Python interpreter major version should be the same as that of "
"Pwntools. However, most GDBs use Python 3 nowadays."
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.gdb:64
msgid ""
"Different minor versions are allowed as long as no incompatible values "
"are sent in either direction. See "
"https://rpyc.readthedocs.io/en/latest/install.html#cross-interpreter-"
"compatibility for more information."
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.gdb:69
msgid "Use"
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.gdb:75
msgid "in order to check your GDB's Python version."
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.gdb:76
msgid ""
"If your GDB uses a different Python interpreter than Pwntools (for "
"example, because you run Pwntools out of a virtualenv), you should "
"install ``rpyc`` package into its ``sys.path``. Use"
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.gdb:84
msgid "in order to check whether this is necessary."
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.gdb:85
msgid "Only local processes are supported."
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.gdb:86
msgid ""
"It is not possible to tell whether ``gdb.execute('continue')`` will be "
"executed synchronously or asynchronously (in gdbscripts it is always "
"synchronous). Therefore it is recommended to use either the explicitly "
"synchronous :func:`pwnlib.gdb.Gdb.continue_and_wait` or the explicitly "
"asynchronous :func:`pwnlib.gdb.Gdb.continue_nowait` instead."
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.gdb:93
msgid "Tips and Troubleshooting"
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.gdb:96
msgid "``NOPTRACE`` magic argument"
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.gdb:98
msgid "It's quite cumbersom to comment and un-comment lines containing `attach`."
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.gdb:100
msgid ""
"You can cause these lines to be a no-op by running your script with the "
"``NOPTRACE`` argument appended, or with ``PWNLIB_NOPTRACE=1`` in the "
"environment."
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.gdb:111
msgid "Kernel Yama ptrace_scope"
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.gdb:113
msgid ""
"The Linux kernel v3.4 introduced a security mechanism called "
"``ptrace_scope``, which is intended to prevent processes from debugging "
"eachother unless there is a direct parent-child relationship."
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.gdb:117
msgid ""
"This causes some issues with the normal Pwntools workflow, since the "
"process hierarchy looks like this:"
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.gdb:125
msgid "Note that ``python`` is the parent of ``target``, not ``gdb``."
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.gdb:127
msgid ""
"In order to avoid this being a problem, Pwntools uses the function "
"``prctl(PR_SET_PTRACER, PR_SET_PTRACER_ANY)``.  This disables Yama for "
"any processes launched by Pwntools via :class:`.process` or via "
":meth:`.ssh.process`."
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.gdb:132
msgid ""
"Older versions of Pwntools did not perform the ``prctl`` step, and "
"required that the Yama security feature was disabled systemwide, which "
"requires ``root`` access."
msgstr ""

